<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tang Dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tang China, Tang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tang Dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 618 to 907 and is often regarded as a high point of Chinese civilisation. Its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> was a cosmopolitan centre linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, and its institutions, law and culture influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. It interacted closely with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, and its collapse was followed by fragmentation and eventual reunification under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty.jpg" alt="Tang Dynasty" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kanguole · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tang_outline_map,_661.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
